1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,072
Lesson 11.3.

2
00:00:02,452 --> 00:00:05,559
Thermalizers: compiling
stochastic programs.

3
00:00:06,683 --> 00:00:10,518
Torx describes a stochastic
program as a circuit of gates.

4
00:00:10,898 --> 00:00:14,213
But the Z1 chip does not
execute arbitrary gates: it only

5
00:00:14,213 --> 00:00:17,470
knows how to sample Ising
models with local connections.

6
00:00:17,850 --> 00:00:20,570
How is a program translated
into what the chip can do?

7
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:25,123
Extropic's companion paper,
thermalizing stochastic programs,

8
00:00:25,123 --> 00:00:29,296
proposes compiling each gate
into a small energy-based model.

9
00:00:30,417 --> 00:00:31,593
The idea is clamping.

10
00:00:31,973 --> 00:00:36,497
We take an energy-based model with
input, hidden and output spins.

11
00:00:36,877 --> 00:00:41,958
We clamp the inputs to x, let the
rest thermalize and read the outputs.

12
00:00:42,338 --> 00:00:46,205
The distribution of the outputs
given the input is a transition

13
00:00:46,205 --> 00:00:49,521
kernel: the hidden spins are
marginalized, never read.

14
00:00:49,901 --> 00:00:53,331
If we add to the energy any
function of the input alone,

15
00:00:53,331 --> 00:00:56,394
the kernel does not change:
it is a gauge freedom.

16
00:00:57,517 --> 00:01:03,118
To compile, we minimize the Kullback-Leibler
divergence from the target kernel to the

17
00:01:03,118 --> 00:01:07,928
model kernel, averaged over inputs:
it is conditional maximum likelihood.

18
00:01:08,308 --> 00:01:12,060
The gradient has two phases,
as in contrastive divergence.

19
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:16,652
In the positive phase we clamp input
and output and sample the hidden spins.

20
00:01:17,032 --> 00:01:19,651
In the negative phase
we clamp only the input.

21
00:01:20,031 --> 00:01:22,181
Each gate is trained separately.

22
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:27,928
We test it ourselves: a random target
kernel from two bits to two bits,

23
00:01:27,928 --> 00:01:32,506
compiled into a Boltzmann machine
with exact gradients by enumeration.

24
00:01:32,886 --> 00:01:36,719
Without hidden spins, the total
variation distance stays at

25
00:01:36,719 --> 00:01:40,292
0.22: a pairwise Ising model
cannot express the kernel.

26
00:01:40,672 --> 00:01:45,358
With one hidden spin, 0.012;
with two, practically zero.

27
00:01:45,738 --> 00:01:51,750
And if we cap the strength of the
couplings, as in a real chip, the

28
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:57,403
error rises: 0.2 with a cap of
0.25, 0.003 with a cap of three.

29
00:01:58,517 --> 00:02:01,960
A deep program chains
many compiled kernels.

30
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:06,884
By the chain rule, the divergence
over trajectories is the sum of the

31
00:02:06,884 --> 00:02:11,494
errors of each kernel, and any final
readout has an error less than or

32
00:02:11,494 --> 00:02:15,050
equal: in the worst case it
grows linearly with depth.

33
00:02:15,430 --> 00:02:18,145
But if the kernels contract,
the error saturates.

34
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:23,432
We see it: with an almost
deterministic program, the error

35
00:02:23,432 --> 00:02:28,340
goes from 0.02 to 0.31 in
thirty steps; with a contracting

36
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:32,656
program, it stays at 0.003,
under the bound of 0.1.

37
00:02:33,783 --> 00:02:36,668
The paper demonstrates the framework in

38
00:02:36,668 --> 00:02:40,439
simulation with THRML, on
a model of the Z1 device.

39
00:02:40,819 --> 00:02:46,348
In a three-body Ising model compiled
with hidden spins, the stationary

40
00:02:46,348 --> 00:02:51,245
error drops from 0.46 to 0.024
as the coupling cap is relaxed.

41
00:02:51,625 --> 00:02:57,735
In a Gaussian circuit for experiment
design, the compiled program achieves an

42
00:02:57,735 --> 00:03:03,289
error of 0.259, equal to the exact
posterior, against 0.305 at random.

43
00:03:03,669 --> 00:03:07,139
The authors warn that every
compiled program is approximate

44
00:03:07,139 --> 00:03:10,373
and that there is no validation
on a physical chip yet.

45
00:03:11,483 --> 00:03:15,537
The workflow: write the program
in torx, compile each gate into a

46
00:03:15,537 --> 00:03:19,903
clamped model, distribute hidden
spins and coupling caps, and validate

47
00:03:19,903 --> 00:03:23,832
first each gate and then the
measure that matters for the task.

48
00:03:24,212 --> 00:03:29,053
A final tuning over trajectories can
help, taking care not to reward cheating.

49
00:03:30,183 --> 00:03:32,843
Common mistakes: expecting
a pairwise Ising model

50
00:03:32,843 --> 00:03:35,176
without hidden spins
to express any kernel.

51
00:03:35,556 --> 00:03:38,847
Ignoring the coupling caps
and topology of the chip.

52
00:03:39,227 --> 00:03:41,354
Relying only on the per-gate error.

53
00:03:41,734 --> 00:03:45,229
And presenting a simulated
compilation as hardware evidence.

54
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:50,524
In summary, a thermalizer realizes
a stochastic kernel by clamping

55
00:03:50,524 --> 00:03:54,319
the inputs of an energy-based
model and reading its outputs.

56
00:03:54,699 --> 00:03:58,132
It is compiled by minimizing
a divergence with positive and

57
00:03:58,132 --> 00:04:01,332
negative phases, and hidden
spins give it expressivity.

58
00:04:01,712 --> 00:04:05,080
Errors add up along the
program, linearly in the

59
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:08,518
worst case, and saturate
if the kernels contract.

60
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:12,802
To practice: write the
kernel of a clamped model.

61
00:04:13,182 --> 00:04:14,928
Explain the gauge freedom.

62
00:04:15,308 --> 00:04:17,103
Name the phases of the gradient.

63
00:04:17,483 --> 00:04:20,711
And bound the stationary error
of a contracting program.
